1
00:00:05,972 --> 00:00:10,010
So obviously, all of us are very
used to thinking about the fact

2
00:00:10,010 --> 00:00:13,413
that we have to think about
data analysis at the same time

3
00:00:13,413 --> 00:00:16,116
that we're thinking through
experimental design.

4
00:00:16,116 --> 00:00:19,819
This is equally so,
obviously, in the context

5
00:00:19,819 --> 00:00:22,122
of computational modeling.

6
00:00:22,122 --> 00:00:24,524
It's not impossible to
take an experiment that

7
00:00:24,524 --> 00:00:27,193
was designed not for
computational modeling,

8
00:00:27,193 --> 00:00:29,596
maybe for statistical
analyzes, and develop

9
00:00:29,596 --> 00:00:31,297
a computational model for it.

10
00:00:31,297 --> 00:00:34,167
But a lot of the
experimental considerations

11
00:00:34,167 --> 00:00:37,070
that get optimized for
statistical analyzes

12
00:00:37,070 --> 00:00:41,608
don't necessarily translate
perfectly to modeling.

13
00:00:41,608 --> 00:00:45,412
And so I just wanted
to bring this up.

14
00:00:45,412 --> 00:00:50,617
So one of the things
that really is

15
00:00:50,617 --> 00:00:53,720
very different is that,
in statistical analyzes,

16
00:00:53,720 --> 00:00:57,557
often what you're
looking at are values.

17
00:00:57,557 --> 00:01:01,461
Are values higher or lower in
some statistically significant

18
00:01:01,461 --> 00:01:02,929
way?

19
00:01:02,929 --> 00:01:06,399
And in the context of
computational modeling,

20
00:01:06,399 --> 00:01:09,502
what you're going to be doing
is creating an ODE system.

21
00:01:09,502 --> 00:01:11,204
So this is a dynamical
system that's

22
00:01:11,204 --> 00:01:13,406
going to have to
evolve over time.

23
00:01:13,406 --> 00:01:16,910
In order for you to
create this system,

24
00:01:16,910 --> 00:01:21,314
you're going to want to be
able to test that the model is

25
00:01:21,314 --> 00:01:22,348
actually accurate.

26
00:01:22,348 --> 00:01:25,051
And so you want to
think about it as, OK,

27
00:01:25,051 --> 00:01:28,021
what ideally is my
model producing?

28
00:01:28,021 --> 00:01:31,291
And is my experiment
setup up to actually test

29
00:01:31,291 --> 00:01:33,593
that this model is correct?

30
00:01:33,593 --> 00:01:36,696
So if you go back to this
glucose insulin model

31
00:01:36,696 --> 00:01:39,632
that we started
with, you'll notice

32
00:01:39,632 --> 00:01:43,169
that if you just simply
think about values,

33
00:01:43,169 --> 00:01:47,440
many of these
different disorders

34
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:51,111
look potentially functionally
identical, especially

35
00:01:51,111 --> 00:01:53,880
if you consider just
a single time point.

36
00:01:53,880 --> 00:01:57,517
So if you're looking
really just here,

37
00:01:57,517 --> 00:02:00,253
these two are going
to look very similar.

38
00:02:00,253 --> 00:02:02,489
And it's only when you
consider the dynamics

39
00:02:02,489 --> 00:02:04,724
that these disorders
start to look different.

40
00:02:04,724 --> 00:02:08,394
So the first thing to mention
is that, even though it's

41
00:02:08,394 --> 00:02:10,864
very common to take
a single measurement,

42
00:02:10,864 --> 00:02:15,135
in the context of developing
a computational neuroscience

43
00:02:15,135 --> 00:02:17,370
model, what you're
going to want are

44
00:02:17,370 --> 00:02:22,709
to collect continuous
measures ideally

45
00:02:22,709 --> 00:02:25,979
over multiple components
of the circuit.

46
00:02:25,979 --> 00:02:29,082
So at the bare minimum,
if you have something

47
00:02:29,082 --> 00:02:31,284
that involves, let's say,
a negative feedback loop,

48
00:02:31,284 --> 00:02:33,553
you would definitely
need to collect a signal

49
00:02:33,553 --> 00:02:36,956
at both the excitatory and
also the inhibitory components

50
00:02:36,956 --> 00:02:38,758
of that negative feedback loop.

51
00:02:38,758 --> 00:02:40,760
So I know this is
challenging for people,

52
00:02:40,760 --> 00:02:42,729
for example, who are
doing electrophysiology

53
00:02:42,729 --> 00:02:46,132
and for whom inserting an
electrode is a very big deal.

54
00:02:46,132 --> 00:02:48,234
But picking those
areas strategically

55
00:02:48,234 --> 00:02:50,370
becomes very important.

56
00:02:50,370 --> 00:02:55,141
Even better if you can measure
at more than two places

57
00:02:55,141 --> 00:02:57,777
and actually as many
places as possible,

58
00:02:57,777 --> 00:03:00,880
the better the coverage
you have over every one

59
00:03:00,880 --> 00:03:05,451
of these components,
the better you're

60
00:03:05,451 --> 00:03:10,323
going to be able to resolve the
dynamics and also causality.

61
00:03:10,323 --> 00:03:13,626
The second point is--

62
00:03:13,626 --> 00:03:14,627
well, OK.

63
00:03:14,627 --> 00:03:18,965
So in line with this, you see
that just measuring one value

64
00:03:18,965 --> 00:03:22,068
here is actually not going
to be enough to discriminate

65
00:03:22,068 --> 00:03:23,203
between these disorders.

66
00:03:23,203 --> 00:03:27,907
So you want, again, to measure
across different components.

67
00:03:27,907 --> 00:03:30,443
You want the dynamics.

68
00:03:30,443 --> 00:03:34,647
And also, you need to
consider that because you're

69
00:03:34,647 --> 00:03:38,384
going to be focusing very
deeply on the dynamics,

70
00:03:38,384 --> 00:03:42,288
averaging time series can
be potentially a problem

71
00:03:42,288 --> 00:03:46,326
because you're going to wash out
your dynamics to some degree.

72
00:03:46,326 --> 00:03:48,628
And so for that
reason, if possible,

73
00:03:48,628 --> 00:03:52,799
you want to maximize your SNR,
your Signal-to-Noise Ratio,

74
00:03:52,799 --> 00:03:59,339
over long data rather than
low SNR over short data.

75
00:03:59,339 --> 00:04:01,975
And that actually goes against
some of the principles that

76
00:04:01,975 --> 00:04:05,245
are used in neuroimaging
these huge data sets,

77
00:04:05,245 --> 00:04:11,084
like UK Biobank, where you
have very short resting state

78
00:04:11,084 --> 00:04:15,288
data but over huge amounts
of huge numbers of people.

79
00:04:15,288 --> 00:04:18,725
And the assumption is
that having lots of people

80
00:04:18,725 --> 00:04:23,896
makes up for the fact that the
data quality for each individual

81
00:04:23,896 --> 00:04:25,031
is not as good.

82
00:04:28,635 --> 00:04:30,970
The other thing you
might want to consider

83
00:04:30,970 --> 00:04:34,874
is that one of the advantages
of computational modeling

84
00:04:34,874 --> 00:04:38,077
is you can design what I
call a critical experiment.

85
00:04:38,077 --> 00:04:39,512
What you want to
be able to do is

86
00:04:39,512 --> 00:04:42,849
to set up your model to
consider potentially competing

87
00:04:42,849 --> 00:04:44,017
hypotheses.

88
00:04:44,017 --> 00:04:47,253
So very often in
neuroscience, there

89
00:04:47,253 --> 00:04:53,860
are many competing ideas about
how the circuit is set up,

90
00:04:53,860 --> 00:04:55,528
what their functions are.

91
00:04:55,528 --> 00:04:57,864
And the beauty of
modeling is that you

92
00:04:57,864 --> 00:05:01,167
can model each one of
those competing hypotheses

93
00:05:01,167 --> 00:05:05,038
and ask what their outputs
would look like dynamically

94
00:05:05,038 --> 00:05:09,676
and then see where those
outputs maximally diverge

95
00:05:09,676 --> 00:05:13,479
and then design the experiment
to measure exactly where they

96
00:05:13,479 --> 00:05:16,115
maximally diverge so
that you can discriminate

97
00:05:16,115 --> 00:05:18,184
between those
competing hypotheses.

98
00:05:18,184 --> 00:05:21,521
This is a very powerful
way to use models.

99
00:05:21,521 --> 00:05:25,058
So again, not even
if you say, OK, I

100
00:05:25,058 --> 00:05:27,226
don't have enough
information about the system

101
00:05:27,226 --> 00:05:30,630
to be sure about how
this model is set up,

102
00:05:30,630 --> 00:05:33,933
even being able to set up a
provisional circuit with lots

103
00:05:33,933 --> 00:05:36,736
of different ways of
doing it, in principle,

104
00:05:36,736 --> 00:05:38,571
you could look at all
the combinatorial ways

105
00:05:38,571 --> 00:05:41,607
that the circuit could be set up
and ask what those outputs would

106
00:05:41,607 --> 00:05:43,476
be and then let
the data tell you

107
00:05:43,476 --> 00:05:48,081
which of those configurations
are compatible with the data

108
00:05:48,081 --> 00:05:49,315
and which ones are not.

109
00:05:52,852 --> 00:05:56,789
And then the other
advantage of the model,

110
00:05:56,789 --> 00:05:59,759
from that perspective,
is that you can

111
00:05:59,759 --> 00:06:01,928
perturb the circuit in silico.

112
00:06:01,928 --> 00:06:04,097
You can ask what
would happen if I

113
00:06:04,097 --> 00:06:08,868
were to stimulate the circuit
here versus here versus here.

114
00:06:08,868 --> 00:06:10,837
How would it affect
the dynamics?

115
00:06:10,837 --> 00:06:14,340
So again, you can do these
in-silico experiments

116
00:06:14,340 --> 00:06:17,410
that then can create
testable hypotheses which

117
00:06:17,410 --> 00:06:18,444
can then be tested.

118
00:06:23,282 --> 00:06:25,918
My last point is
that if you're going

119
00:06:25,918 --> 00:06:30,590
to take advantage of the
multiscale capabilities

120
00:06:30,590 --> 00:06:32,992
of neural blocks,
you want to make sure

121
00:06:32,992 --> 00:06:36,062
that you validate these
by also collecting data

122
00:06:36,062 --> 00:06:40,433
across multiple scales
and ideally simultaneously

123
00:06:40,433 --> 00:06:43,369
so that then you can develop
the transfer functions that

124
00:06:43,369 --> 00:06:47,407
allow you to ask, how do you
get from the bottom layer

125
00:06:47,407 --> 00:06:51,310
to the next layer to the
next layer to the next layer?

126
00:06:51,310 --> 00:06:54,547
So again, I'm not saying that
these are easy experiments

127
00:06:54,547 --> 00:06:58,284
to conduct, but it's something
to consider if your ultimate aim

128
00:06:58,284 --> 00:07:04,056
is to create a rigorous model.

